1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,200
Beyond the amber waves of grain, over a million dollars worth of equipment sits idle.

2
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:12,820
These are the machines of the Meisner family, one of 700 crews, known as custom harvesters,

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,660
who haul in nearly half of the United States wheat crop.

4
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:20,260
Each year, they race against time to gather the delicate grain.

5
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:26,220
Once the crop is ready, you need to be right on top of it, all within a week's time.

6
00:00:26,221 --> 00:00:31,260
and we actually follow the harvest of the wheat as it matures north.

7
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:36,340
The responsibility of feeding the world weighs heavily on their shoulders.

8
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:39,440
Any holdup at harvest could be catastrophic.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,020
At their stop in Elk City, Oklahoma,

10
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:47,420
the Meisner crew has been delayed by a recent rainstorm

11
00:00:47,421 --> 00:00:51,320
and this custom harvester is desperate to get rolling again.

12
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:54,060
When these machines of mine are not running,

13
00:00:54,061 --> 00:00:56,300
It's costing me $1,500 an hour.

14
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:59,600
We've been sitting for a couple of days here,

15
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:01,960
but we've had a good strong wind here,

16
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:04,440
so hopefully this wheat is going to run now.

17
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:08,700
But we'll just get out here and see what we got for a sample

18
00:01:08,701 --> 00:01:10,420
and check things out.

19
00:01:11,540 --> 00:01:12,980
Before harvest can begin,

20
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:17,020
the moisture level of the wheat must be under 13.5 percent,

21
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,380
any higher, and the wheat could spoil.

22
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,000
The head, or spike of the wheat,

23
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:24,180
contains the kernels, or grain,

24
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,160
which are the only part of the plant used for human food.

25
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:29,200
We're going to grab a few heads here,

26
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:33,360
and the object is we're going to put them in my palm here,

27
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:36,660
and we're going to thrash those kernels out of that head

28
00:01:36,661 --> 00:01:38,480
and then bite on them.

29
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:42,520
And you can hear them crack, pop,

30
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:44,380
it's going to be ready to go.

31
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:47,520
We got a couple of soft ones,

32
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:49,400
but it really tastes pretty darn good.

33
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:51,380
We're going to go. We're going to try it.

34
00:01:51,381 --> 00:01:55,720
My crew's anxious to start cutting wheat, but we're going to get going here.

35
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,060
Okay, Emma, get them organized.

36
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:00,700
Rank them up.

37
00:02:00,701 --> 00:02:01,401
We're going to cut wheat.

38
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:05,840
Copy the combines.

39
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,020
Go ahead and raise all your heads up.

40
00:02:08,021 --> 00:02:10,500
Pull in and just make a nice gradual sweep.

41
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:16,380
The vast majority of the crop they harvest is hard red winter wheat,

42
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:19,900
which ultimately becomes the staff of life, bread.

43
00:02:19,901 --> 00:02:25,400
Harvest starts in Texas every May and wraps up in Canada in September.

44
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:32,060
By that time, the Meisner team alone will harvest more than one million bushels,

45
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,520
enough wheat to feed nearly half a million people for a year.

46
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,620
That's if the weather doesn't best them.

47
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:41,200
We've been in hell.

48
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,960
We have been in the field with a tornado within a quarter of a mile of us.

49
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,280
We do a lot of praying to the good Lord.

50
00:02:47,281 --> 00:02:50,140
and their machines don't break down.

51
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:52,340
It's a family operation.

52
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,720
When my wife and I started, we had two combines.

53
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:56,300
She drove one, I drove one.

54
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,000
We have six children, four daughters and two sons.

55
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:01,360
Every one of the kids have grown up on the floor of a combine.

56
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:05,520
Now their operation has grown to five combines.

57
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:09,600
It's equipment and manpower that many wheat farmers can't afford.

58
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:13,700
They can actually get our wheat out in three days,

59
00:03:13,701 --> 00:03:16,740
whereas it would take us almost two weeks with one machine.

60
00:03:16,741 --> 00:03:22,020
Being a wheat farmer probably is being one of the biggest gamblers there is.

61
00:03:22,021 --> 00:03:28,640
You've got a crop worth hundreds of thousands in the field that can be wiped out in the matter of 30 minutes.

62
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:33,420
These gamblers depend on the custom harvester to be their ace in the hole.

63
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:39,160
Though custom harvesting with combines has been around for more than 100 years,

64
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:42,280
the industry really took hold in the 1940s.

65
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,780
But some crews became hasty and dishonest.

66
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:48,860
Farmers tagged them harvest gypsies.

67
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:52,320
This perception stuck until the 1960s,

68
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:54,280
when it became a reputable profession.

69
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,240
Today's custom cutters are the dependable warriors of the wheat harvest.

70
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,880
They can clean out 100 acres per combine per day.

71
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:10,320
Emma Meisner has been behind the wheel of a combine since she was 10 years old.

72
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:15,540
At first, when you get into it, you know, having driven before,

73
00:04:15,541 --> 00:04:16,940
It's really nerve wracking.

74
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:22,580
There's a lot of things to watch at one time, but it's just fun.

75
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,580
It's just like mowing the lawn with a 30-foot head.

76
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,360
What girl wouldn't like to drive a huge combine?

77
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:33,600
As each 30-foot-wide, 30,000-pound combine rolls through a field,

78
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:39,080
a spinning reel pulls the weed into a razor-sharp sickle bar that cuts the stalks.

79
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:41,780
This is very, very sharp.

80
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,400
It will cut anything off that comes in front of it.

81
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:48,420
So this is very, very hardened material.

82
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:52,260
It's a special alloy steel that is heat treated.

83
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:55,640
These knives are not sharpened.

84
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:58,480
They stay sharp for the duration of the life.

85
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:02,940
Once cut, an auger moves the stalks through the feeder house

86
00:05:02,941 --> 00:05:07,340
and into the heart of the machine, where the grain is separated from the stem.

87
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:13,200
The huge force of a cylinder spinning against a concave at up to 960 RPM

88
00:05:13,201 --> 00:05:15,260
knocks the grain off the stem.

89
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:18,340
This process is called threshing.

90
00:05:18,341 --> 00:05:22,040
The separated grain then falls through the grates of the concave

91
00:05:22,041 --> 00:05:25,040
into a cleaning system before it's moved into the holding tank.

92
00:05:25,041 --> 00:05:30,540
Since each tank can only hold about 300 bushels of wheat,

93
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:34,540
the combine must unload on the move nearly every 30 minutes.

94
00:05:34,541 --> 00:05:40,540
I go from combine to combine and take their wheat and put it

95
00:05:40,541 --> 00:05:44,540
in the grain cart and then bring it to the truck so the truck

96
00:05:44,541 --> 00:05:47,280
So the truck can bring the wheat to market.

97
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:52,620
The semis make multiple trips back and forth to the grain elevator each day.

98
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:57,660
A holdup with the trucks could mean serious setbacks in the field.

99
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:01,860
Sometimes there will be no line of trucks here.

100
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:03,540
You can just pull up on the scale.

101
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,360
Sometimes it'll be like this.

102
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:11,080
I could say we could be here at least a good hour.

103
00:06:11,081 --> 00:06:16,080
Luckily, we've got a couple other trucks back at the field.

104
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:18,580
This is no 9-to-5 job.

105
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:21,520
We can run 24-7 if we needed to.

106
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:24,620
And I have run all night long, knowing that if there

107
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:26,760
would be a rainstorm coming out in the morning,

108
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:29,300
we would cut right till we would get rained on.

109
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:33,200
We stay generally on the farmer's place.

110
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:35,040
We have three travel trailers.

111
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:38,440
We have a main house where all the meals are served out of.

112
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:39,500
In line.

113
00:06:39,501 --> 00:06:42,140
My wife is a fantastic cook.

114
00:06:42,141 --> 00:06:45,000
She can cook anything and everything on a grill.

115
00:06:45,001 --> 00:06:46,400
You name it, she can do it all.

116
00:06:46,401 --> 00:06:51,740
And then we have a crew trailer, which all the guys are in.

117
00:06:51,741 --> 00:06:54,340
This is our home away from home.

118
00:06:54,341 --> 00:06:55,440
It's kind of small.

119
00:06:55,441 --> 00:06:58,760
We got anywhere from three to seven guys living in here,

120
00:06:58,761 --> 00:07:01,060
kind of depending on the harvest year.

121
00:07:01,061 --> 00:07:02,960
Back here we have three bunks.

122
00:07:02,961 --> 00:07:06,160
They kind of stack them in here, kind of tight.

123
00:07:06,161 --> 00:07:09,100
The top two each have a window.

124
00:07:09,101 --> 00:07:11,100
The bottom one does not.

125
00:07:11,101 --> 00:07:13,100
We like to refer to that as the burrow.

126
00:07:13,101 --> 00:07:16,100
And then this is where the other three guys sleep.

127
00:07:16,101 --> 00:07:19,100
This bed down here is actually where I sleep.

128
00:07:19,101 --> 00:07:24,100
Not a small guy, so the easiest way to get in the bed is kind of lay on the floor and you just kind of roll in.

129
00:07:24,101 --> 00:07:26,100
And then when you get up in the morning, you just kind of roll out.

130
00:07:26,101 --> 00:07:27,100
It's pretty comfortable.

131
00:07:27,101 --> 00:07:32,100
I mean, by the time you get done working 12, 14-hour days, whatever it is, you're so beat.

132
00:07:32,101 --> 00:07:34,100
You just want to go to sleep.

133
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:38,500
The work is very, very hard, but it's so gratifying

134
00:07:38,501 --> 00:07:43,080
because people entrust you with such large responsibility.

135
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:47,620
The job we have just completed, we did well over 3,000 acres

136
00:07:47,621 --> 00:07:49,960
in just over a week's time.

137
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:54,540
We almost held off a million dollars' worth of wheat.

138
00:07:56,280 --> 00:07:58,140
But there's no time to rest.

139
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:00,860
The Meisners must roll onto the next wheat farm

140
00:08:00,861 --> 00:08:02,580
before Mother Nature strikes again.

141
00:08:02,581 --> 00:08:09,580
They still have 400 fields in three more states to harvest before their work ends.

142
00:08:09,581 --> 00:08:14,580
The combine caravan has become commonplace in the Great Flames.

143
00:08:14,581 --> 00:08:22,580
But just 200 years ago, farmers could only rely on manpower to harvest their crop.

144
00:08:22,581 --> 00:08:29,580
Cutting wheat was done solely by hand, with a long, sharp cutting blade called a scythe.

145
00:08:29,581 --> 00:08:36,260
Back in those days, the fields were nothing compared with the size of these fields.

146
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:40,180
You'd have a heart attack before you got a field like this cut.

147
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:44,500
It was a little slow, but it was good, honest, hard work.

148
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:48,080
But mussel can't compete with machinery.

149
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:52,660
While a farmer and his scythe can cut maybe two acres of wheat per day,

150
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:58,800
today's modern combines can cut the same two acres in 12 minutes or less.

151
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:06,080
And in just nine seconds, a combine harvests enough wheat to make 70 loaves of bread.

152
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:10,000
And the world demands plenty of bread.

153
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:14,740
Next to corn, wheat is the second most cultivated cereal grain in the world.

154
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:19,620
Wheat is grown virtually everywhere, and in any given month of the year,

155
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,660
wheat is being harvested somewhere in the world.

156
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:26,480
More than 60 million acres of wheat are harvested each year in the United States.

157
00:09:26,481 --> 00:09:34,700
out of the 42 wheat growing states kansas typically ranks number one in fact nearly

158
00:09:34,701 --> 00:09:42,200
one-fifth of all u.s wheat is kansas grown north dakota is a close second kansas grows about 10

159
00:09:42,201 --> 00:09:47,960
million acres per year of wheat which is a lot of wheat but the world as a whole grows about 500

160
00:09:47,961 --> 00:09:52,880
million acres per year of wheat the number one producer in the world surprisingly is actually

161
00:09:52,881 --> 00:09:59,100
China. China produces more than 90 million tons of wheat each year and India

162
00:09:59,101 --> 00:10:04,100
generates 70 million tons. The United States 60 million tons ranks third,

163
00:10:04,101 --> 00:10:11,640
followed by Russia, France and Canada. Even so, the United States is the largest

164
00:10:11,641 --> 00:10:17,660
wheat exporter. Nearly 50% of the crop is shipped overseas each year. Wheat

165
00:10:17,661 --> 00:10:22,160
provides approximately 20% of the food needs of the world. One acre will produce

166
00:10:22,161 --> 00:10:25,760
enough wheat to feed 9,000 people for one day.

167
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:28,660
The Port of Portland in Oregon exports the largest

168
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:31,460
amount of United States wheat.

169
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:34,600
Four million tons pass through Columbia Grain's Terminal 5

170
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:37,500
export elevator each year.

171
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:40,520
The grain that we're going to get here is going to go all

172
00:10:40,620 --> 00:10:41,520
over the world.

173
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:45,020
Our primary customers are the Far Eastern flour millers,

174
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:48,660
Japan being a very big customer.

175
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:51,860
The facility unloads about 75% of the wheat by rail

176
00:10:51,861 --> 00:10:58,860
Each one of these cars really takes only about five minutes to unload.

177
00:10:58,861 --> 00:11:05,860
The weed is going to fall into the rail receiving pit here, and then it's going to access some

178
00:11:05,861 --> 00:11:11,860
conveying systems to get to bucket elevator legs, where we'll take it to the top of the elevator,

179
00:11:11,861 --> 00:11:16,860
and then distribute it for storage into one of the storage silos.

180
00:11:16,861 --> 00:11:24,660
With a storage capacity of 100,000 tons, the inventory may be emptied and refilled up to five times each month.

181
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:33,680
Two conveyor belts carry the grain at 900 tons per hour from the silos out to the ship for transport.

182
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:40,160
We're loading hard red winter wheat, and this hatch is going to hold about 10,000 tons,

183
00:11:40,161 --> 00:11:45,660
which is equivalent to the size of one of those 110-car shuttle trains.

184
00:11:45,661 --> 00:11:52,400
We started loading this vessel about 2 in the morning today, and it'll take us a couple days to finish it.

185
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:58,420
But this worldwide staple wasn't always so widely distributed.

186
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:06,700
Weed originated as a wild grass food more than 16,000 years ago in Southwest Asia,

187
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:11,060
in the area known as the Fertile Crescent, modern-day Syria and Iraq.

188
00:12:11,061 --> 00:12:16,060
Nomads gathered the so-called wild einkorn wheat,

189
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:19,060
separated the kernels, and ate them raw.

190
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:23,100
Around 7,000 BC, they discovered that planting the grain yielded

191
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:25,000
yet more seeds.

192
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:28,620
And it was these seeds of wheat that became the seeds of

193
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:29,920
civilization.

194
00:12:30,020 --> 00:12:33,120
People no longer were forced to migrate around looking for

195
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:34,520
wild stands of wheat.

196
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:37,120
It really was the genesis of our civilization that people were

197
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:39,560
able to become full-time farmers.

198
00:12:39,561 --> 00:12:44,940
By 5000 BC, wheat farming had spread throughout Asia and into Europe and North

199
00:12:44,941 --> 00:12:51,440
Africa. Soon farmers began to cook and grind the grains. When people first

200
00:12:51,441 --> 00:12:59,560
started grinding whole grains into flour, this was as advanced as it got. By

201
00:12:59,561 --> 00:13:04,940
adding water to the coarse ground flour, farmers could create porridge. And many

202
00:13:04,941 --> 00:13:08,780
attribute the origin of bread making to a serendipitous event when someone left

203
00:13:08,781 --> 00:13:10,020
out in the hot sun.

204
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:15,620
It's a primitive method that remains tried and true.

205
00:13:15,621 --> 00:13:18,620
So we put that flatbread in the oven about 20 minutes ago.

206
00:13:18,621 --> 00:13:20,020
Let's see what we have.

207
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:27,460
Well, there it is.

208
00:13:27,461 --> 00:13:29,540
Nicely browned on the sides.

209
00:13:29,541 --> 00:13:30,540
Pretty light.

210
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:36,040
Smelled weedy.

211
00:13:36,041 --> 00:13:38,780
Let's see what happens when we break it down.

212
00:13:38,781 --> 00:13:39,481
open.

213
00:13:39,482 --> 00:13:48,620
Very dark, a little moist, probably could have been thinner, but looks good enough to eat.

214
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:53,458
A little crunchy, tastes right.

215
00:13:53,459 --> 00:13:58,140
I can see why people did this for thousands of years.

216
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:03,820
By chance, wild airborne yeast found its way into an ancient wheat mixture,

217
00:14:03,821 --> 00:14:05,940
creating leavened bread.

218
00:14:07,220 --> 00:14:09,060
And the rest is delicious history.

219
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:13,480
Today, more than 70% of harvested wheat

220
00:14:13,481 --> 00:14:14,680
is used to make bread.

221
00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:20,140
Whether baking baguettes, ciabatta, or sourdough,

222
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:23,080
the staff of life is fashioned from wheat.

223
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:26,100
So is pasta.

224
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:29,420
But if Marco Polo didn't bring it to Italy,

225
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:30,780
who did?

226
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:33,360
Ooh, hot!

227
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:40,820
Among the grains found in food products, wheat takes the cake and makes the cake.

228
00:14:40,821 --> 00:14:42,820
It's in more than 3,000 foods.

229
00:14:42,821 --> 00:14:49,360
But before these items make it to the supermarket shelf, wheat undergoes a high-tech journey

230
00:14:49,361 --> 00:14:53,620
from field to flour.

231
00:14:53,621 --> 00:14:59,700
The Conagra Mills plant in Decatur, Alabama, grinds more than 1.6 million pounds of flour

232
00:14:59,701 --> 00:15:01,700
every day.

233
00:15:01,701 --> 00:15:09,440
About 50% of our wheat comes in via barge, and this particular barge has 50,000 bushels

234
00:15:09,441 --> 00:15:14,820
of grain on it, which to put in perspective would be roughly 3.6 million loaves of bread.

235
00:15:14,821 --> 00:15:19,020
We have two power shovels that are pulling the wheat towards the center of the barge

236
00:15:19,021 --> 00:15:23,620
where we have an elevator leg that pulls the wheat up to the conveyor that pulls it back

237
00:15:23,621 --> 00:15:25,880
to our flat storage.

238
00:15:25,881 --> 00:15:30,620
We can unload 6,200 bushels an hour using those shovels.

239
00:15:30,621 --> 00:15:35,420
Each bushel of wheat contains a million kernels.

240
00:15:35,421 --> 00:15:38,020
Each kernel is made up of three parts.

241
00:15:38,021 --> 00:15:41,260
The outer shell called bran, full of fiber.

242
00:15:41,261 --> 00:15:44,600
The embryo called germ, high in protein.

243
00:15:44,601 --> 00:15:48,940
And the nutritive tissue called endosperm, high in starch.

244
00:15:48,941 --> 00:15:53,520
Whole grain flour is made from the entire kernel, while white flour is made from the endosperm

245
00:15:53,521 --> 00:15:54,520
only.

246
00:15:54,521 --> 00:16:00,480
Out of 100 pounds of wheat, we'll make about 75 pounds of refined white flour,

247
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:08,840
about 23 pounds of mill feed, which contains the bran, and about 2 pounds of wheat germ.

248
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:15,800
The process begins with a rigorous cleaning routine to remove any debris from the grain.

249
00:16:16,940 --> 00:16:20,280
From there, the colonels enter one of 28 roller mills.

250
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:23,920
Each roller mill has a slightly different purpose.

251
00:16:23,921 --> 00:16:27,320
The stock comes in the top, comes down through the machine,

252
00:16:27,420 --> 00:16:30,420
it's ground, and discharges the bottom.

253
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:32,720
This is the first brake roller mill.

254
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:34,860
Here, whole wheat comes into the machine,

255
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:36,760
and it's ground for the first time.

256
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:41,040
The rollers shitter the wheat, break it open,

257
00:16:41,140 --> 00:16:42,300
prepare it for sifting.

258
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:49,820
A complex array of pressurized tubes

259
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:52,720
carries the grain from floor to floor.

260
00:16:52,721 --> 00:16:54,920
We're here on the fifth floor of the mill.

261
00:16:54,921 --> 00:16:58,120
The purpose of the sifter is to separate the ground mill

262
00:16:58,121 --> 00:17:00,620
stocks by particle size.

263
00:17:00,621 --> 00:17:04,160
This is done with a nest of sieves inside each sifter

264
00:17:04,161 --> 00:17:05,060
section.

265
00:17:05,061 --> 00:17:07,300
The sifter swings in about a 3 and 1

266
00:17:07,301 --> 00:17:12,080
half inch circle at about 240 RPM.

267
00:17:12,081 --> 00:17:14,180
As the sifter rotates, the sieves

268
00:17:14,181 --> 00:17:17,120
separate the ground grain by size.

269
00:17:17,121 --> 00:17:19,380
The larger particles remain at the top,

270
00:17:19,381 --> 00:17:22,720
while the smaller ones fall to the bottom.

271
00:17:22,721 --> 00:17:25,420
The coarse browning particles will go to brake rolls,

272
00:17:25,800 --> 00:17:28,760
will be ground with roller mills that have teeth,

273
00:17:29,420 --> 00:17:34,460
and the endosperm containing white, fine particles will go to reduction rolls,

274
00:17:34,540 --> 00:17:36,840
which are smooth, but will grind hard into flour.

275
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:44,000
The colonels pass through these rollers and sifters up to 15 times in just 20 minutes

276
00:17:44,001 --> 00:17:46,700
before heading off to packaging as white flour.

277
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:51,600
Though this mechanized process is hundreds of years old,

278
00:17:51,601 --> 00:17:54,500
ConAgra has managed to put a new spin on it.

279
00:17:54,501 --> 00:17:56,600
We came up with a special milling process,

280
00:17:56,601 --> 00:18:00,100
which reduces the bran and the germ particles

281
00:18:00,101 --> 00:18:02,640
to the same particle size as the endosperm.

282
00:18:02,641 --> 00:18:04,640
The so-called ultragrain flour

283
00:18:04,641 --> 00:18:07,640
maintains the nutritional value of whole wheat.

284
00:18:07,641 --> 00:18:09,760
This is a traditional whole wheat bread.

285
00:18:09,761 --> 00:18:13,560
You can see the very visible bran specks in that.

286
00:18:13,561 --> 00:18:16,560
When you look at this 100% ultragrain product,

287
00:18:16,561 --> 00:18:19,560
you can see that it's very much like a white bread product.

288
00:18:19,561 --> 00:18:24,560
It has a very soft texture, and when you eat it,

289
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:27,560
it has the very same mouthfeel as some of the white breads

290
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:29,560
that you find in the marketplace today.

291
00:18:29,660 --> 00:18:33,560
Whether it's ultra-grain, whole wheat, or white,

292
00:18:33,660 --> 00:18:36,560
choosing the right flour for your baked goods is essential.

293
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:40,560
That's because flour can be ground from two types of wheat,

294
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:42,560
hard or soft.

295
00:18:42,660 --> 00:18:46,560
In fact, the secret to wheat's superior bread-making ability

296
00:18:46,561 --> 00:18:52,300
is gluten, a protein that is formed when water is added to wheat flour and mixed

297
00:18:52,301 --> 00:18:59,220
into a dough. The gluten gives bread its volume and structure. It's the key

298
00:18:59,221 --> 00:19:04,500
component in the grain that makes doughs stretchy and holds baked goods together.

299
00:19:04,501 --> 00:19:09,780
Hard wheat contains lots of gluten. These wheats are used to produce bread and

300
00:19:09,781 --> 00:19:14,620
rolls. Soft wheat contains little gluten so the products don't stretch as much

301
00:19:14,621 --> 00:19:22,720
becoming flaky and tender. These wheats are used in crackers, cakes and pastries.

302
00:19:23,560 --> 00:19:28,740
But it takes a special kind of wheat to make the quintessential Italian dish.

303
00:19:28,741 --> 00:19:34,940
Durum is an especially hard wheat variety used to create pasta. Americans

304
00:19:34,941 --> 00:19:41,740
consume more than six billion pounds of pasta each year. New World Pasta is the

305
00:19:41,741 --> 00:19:45,740
the leading dry pasta manufacturer in the United States.

306
00:19:45,741 --> 00:19:47,940
At their plant in Winchester, Virginia,

307
00:19:47,941 --> 00:19:52,880
they make oodles and oodles of noodles, 24-7.

308
00:19:52,881 --> 00:19:56,020
We make over 600,000 pounds of product in a day,

309
00:19:56,021 --> 00:19:59,240
or up to 200 million pounds in a year.

310
00:19:59,241 --> 00:20:02,220
We make several different brands of pasta here,

311
00:20:02,221 --> 00:20:06,060
but our most popular are our Razzoni brands.

312
00:20:06,061 --> 00:20:09,140
Directly behind me is our lasagna production.

313
00:20:09,141 --> 00:20:14,060
We have seven production lines where we can make up to 52 different shapes.

314
00:20:15,500 --> 00:20:21,200
The process starts with taking semolina flour, adding water, mixing it.

315
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:24,820
The mixing process will take anywhere from 15 to 20 minutes.

316
00:20:26,600 --> 00:20:29,720
Semolina is the coarsely ground endosperm of durum wheat.

317
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:34,700
It has two key essential characteristics, which are color and tenacity.

318
00:20:35,340 --> 00:20:38,580
The color comes from the naturally occurring pigments in the wheat.

319
00:20:39,140 --> 00:20:41,000
And that translates into the golden color

320
00:20:41,001 --> 00:20:42,980
that we normally see in pasta.

321
00:20:42,981 --> 00:20:45,380
The tenacity comes from the wheat gluten,

322
00:20:45,381 --> 00:20:47,480
which is the protein in the wheat.

323
00:20:47,481 --> 00:20:52,120
And that provides the strength of the product.

324
00:20:52,121 --> 00:20:54,580
Under Italian law, pasta can only

325
00:20:54,581 --> 00:20:59,420
be made from 100% durum wheat semolina flour and water.

326
00:20:59,421 --> 00:21:01,360
Even though there's not a similar law in the United

327
00:21:01,361 --> 00:21:05,160
States, New World pasta still adheres to Italy's strict pasta

328
00:21:05,161 --> 00:21:07,360
principle.

329
00:21:07,361 --> 00:21:09,660
After mixing, we extrude the product.

330
00:21:10,220 --> 00:21:14,100
During the extrusion process, we are forcing that dough through a dye,

331
00:21:14,300 --> 00:21:16,740
and that dye gives the product a particular shape,

332
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,740
similar to what we did as a kid using Play-Doh.

333
00:21:21,100 --> 00:21:24,320
But these industrial Play-Doh machines aren't kid-friendly.

334
00:21:25,740 --> 00:21:28,520
Razor-sharp blades rotating beneath the metal dyes

335
00:21:28,521 --> 00:21:30,740
slice and dice the pasta to size.

336
00:21:33,160 --> 00:21:36,180
Once cut, the pasta makes its way to massive dryers

337
00:21:36,181 --> 00:21:41,100
that circulate hot, moist air around up to 30,000 pounds of product at a time.

338
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:45,420
Temperatures will reach up to 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

339
00:21:46,040 --> 00:21:49,040
Our short goods products take about three hours to dry,

340
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:52,180
where long goods products take up to five hours to dry.

341
00:21:53,340 --> 00:21:57,440
Packaging is a modern labyrinth of conveyors, buckets, and chutes.

342
00:21:59,520 --> 00:22:02,920
Overflowing with spaghetti, penne, and rotini.

343
00:22:02,921 --> 00:22:06,920
Wheat's long journey to the dinner table as pasta can be

344
00:22:06,921 --> 00:22:09,920
traced as far back as 2000 BC.

345
00:22:09,921 --> 00:22:13,920
But pasta's origins are shrouded in mystery and myth.

346
00:22:13,921 --> 00:22:17,920
Contrary to popular belief, Marco Polo did not bring pasta

347
00:22:17,921 --> 00:22:18,920
to Italy.

348
00:22:18,921 --> 00:22:21,920
Most historians believe it was introduced to Italy during the

349
00:22:21,921 --> 00:22:24,920
Arab conquests of Sicily in the 8th century.

350
00:22:24,921 --> 00:22:29,920
Today, there are more than 600 pasta shapes worldwide.

351
00:22:29,921 --> 00:22:32,620
pasta shapes worldwide.

352
00:22:32,621 --> 00:22:36,640
While Americans eat an average of 20 pounds of pasta annually,

353
00:22:36,641 --> 00:22:38,920
that's only a third of what their Italian counterparts

354
00:22:38,921 --> 00:22:41,100
consume.

355
00:22:41,101 --> 00:22:44,000
Cooking pasta is as simple as boiling water,

356
00:22:44,001 --> 00:22:46,720
but it takes a keen eye to get it just right.

357
00:22:46,721 --> 00:22:48,700
I like it a little al dente.

358
00:22:48,701 --> 00:22:50,680
That means to the tooth.

359
00:22:50,681 --> 00:22:53,360
You can usually tell when it's al dente

360
00:22:53,361 --> 00:22:56,600
by looking at the ends of the pasta,

361
00:22:56,601 --> 00:22:59,440
and you'll see a tiny little white core that's not cooked

362
00:22:59,441 --> 00:23:01,860
as well as the surrounding area of the pasta.

363
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:03,840
And it gives it that unique little bite

364
00:23:03,841 --> 00:23:06,860
and cleanliness when you're eating it.

365
00:23:09,320 --> 00:23:10,020
Perfecto.

366
00:23:10,780 --> 00:23:12,340
Molto bene, as they would say in Italy.

367
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:13,260
It's perfect.

368
00:23:13,920 --> 00:23:16,700
While wheat lovers worldwide are piling on the pasta,

369
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:21,160
others are turning to wheat beer to wash it down.

370
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:26,100
But why is it hazy instead of clear like other beers?

371
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:37,440
beer 35 billion gallons of the frothy brew are sold worldwide each year

372
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:42,480
americans chug down more than 15 million gallons of the stuff each day

373
00:23:45,120 --> 00:23:50,800
barley is the grain used to make most beers but at the widmer brothers brewery in portland oregon

374
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:58,400
wheat has taken over since 1986 the brewery has been home to america's original hefeweizen

375
00:23:58,401 --> 00:23:59,780
or unfiltered wheat beer.

376
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:06,300
It's a brew that first became popular in 15th century Bavaria

377
00:24:06,301 --> 00:24:10,280
and currently makes up almost a quarter of beer sales in Germany.

378
00:24:12,520 --> 00:24:15,480
Wheat beer's unique style is also catching on in America.

379
00:24:18,020 --> 00:24:18,720
Yum.

380
00:24:18,880 --> 00:24:19,680
That's pretty good.

381
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:23,980
Wheat contributes sort of a nice aromatic fresh bread profile,

382
00:24:24,240 --> 00:24:25,640
which we find very appealing.

383
00:24:25,820 --> 00:24:27,200
It also contributes mouthfeel.

384
00:24:27,201 --> 00:24:30,540
It gives the beer more substance on your tongue and on your palate.

385
00:24:32,280 --> 00:24:34,980
Each week, brewing brothers Kurt and Rob Widmer

386
00:24:34,981 --> 00:24:41,140
turn 75,000 pounds of wheat into more than 125,000 gallons of beer.

387
00:24:43,060 --> 00:24:47,640
Last year we brewed about 350,000 barrels out of this facility.

388
00:24:47,900 --> 00:24:49,840
This is kind of the hot end of our brew house,

389
00:24:50,040 --> 00:24:54,360
and we can brew six or seven times a day in a 24-hour period.

390
00:24:54,361 --> 00:25:00,900
The bottom line of brewing beer is to convert the grain sugars into alcohol and carbon dioxide.

391
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:04,940
The process starts with this tank called a mash mixer.

392
00:25:05,440 --> 00:25:08,080
That's where you take malted wheat and soak it in warm water.

393
00:25:09,640 --> 00:25:13,080
Malted wheat is grain that has been soaked in water to the point of germination.

394
00:25:14,440 --> 00:25:18,740
This causes the grain to produce enzymes that convert its starch into sugar.

395
00:25:18,741 --> 00:25:25,220
Even though this brew is hefeweizen, Widmer uses malted barley for half the mixture.

396
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:32,360
The enzymatic power of the grain to convert starch to sugar is less in wheat than barley,

397
00:25:32,520 --> 00:25:34,420
so you really do need the barley there.

398
00:25:35,260 --> 00:25:39,600
The mash mixture spins for an hour at 126 degrees Fahrenheit.

399
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:45,960
And then we ramp that up, ending at about 160 degrees, and then we call mash off,

400
00:25:45,961 --> 00:25:51,400
which is at 170, and that stops the enzymatic conversion of starch to sugar.

401
00:25:52,520 --> 00:25:55,740
The mash is then pumped into a so-called louder ton,

402
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:58,940
in which the solution of water and sugar is called wort,

403
00:25:59,460 --> 00:26:01,680
is drained from the mash through a sieve-like bottom.

404
00:26:02,820 --> 00:26:07,800
A mechanical rotating rake ensures the mash remains fluid throughout the process.

405
00:26:08,460 --> 00:26:13,480
That's about 170 degrees, and it takes about two hours to complete the loudering.

406
00:26:13,481 --> 00:26:18,720
This is a tricky part of the process when you're brewing wheat beer because wheat has a higher protein content

407
00:26:18,721 --> 00:26:23,440
and it tends to, you know, to use a technical term, gum up the works.

408
00:26:24,160 --> 00:26:29,840
And if you're not kind of on top of it all the time, you can have a stuck mash where the liquid won't flow through.

409
00:26:30,160 --> 00:26:35,540
This tank does a great job of making sure that we're able to rinse all the flavor and color and sugar out of the grain.

410
00:26:36,940 --> 00:26:40,860
The remaining wheat and barley head to a local farm to become cattle feed

411
00:26:40,861 --> 00:26:43,720
while the liquid continues onto the kettle for boiling.

412
00:26:44,860 --> 00:26:46,420
So we're done with the grain.

413
00:26:46,560 --> 00:26:48,460
We've just got the sweet grain tea called wort.

414
00:26:49,060 --> 00:26:50,720
And it's here that hops are introduced.

415
00:26:51,160 --> 00:26:52,740
These are baled hops.

416
00:26:52,741 --> 00:26:56,140
And what we do is get them pulverized and pelletized.

417
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:01,660
Hops are flowering plants that give beer its bitterness, aroma, and flavor.

418
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:05,480
Wheat beers tend to have a low amount of hops

419
00:27:05,481 --> 00:27:07,960
and a high concentration of carbon dioxide.

420
00:27:07,961 --> 00:27:13,840
That means a light flavor with lots of bubbles, making it a popular summertime brew.

421
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:17,100
But where does that golden haze come from?

422
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:25,840
There are proteins that we extract from the wheat malt that go all the way through to the glass that we think is an essential part of how the beer should look.

423
00:27:27,220 --> 00:27:32,560
Since Hefeweizen is unfiltered, the proteins from the wheat remain suspended in the end product.

424
00:27:33,480 --> 00:27:36,540
These long molecular chains are difficult to break apart.

425
00:27:36,541 --> 00:27:41,920
creating both a cloudy appearance and the distinctive thick foamy head of the beer.

426
00:27:44,920 --> 00:27:51,100
As a result, a bottle of Hefeweizen offers the same amount of protein and carbohydrates as a slice of bread.

427
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:56,360
Back in the brew house, the wort boils for 75 minutes.

428
00:27:57,420 --> 00:27:59,620
But it's still not beer yet. There's no alcohol present.

429
00:27:59,960 --> 00:28:03,000
But we've set up the absolute perfect conditions for fermentation,

430
00:28:03,700 --> 00:28:05,860
and that's started by adding our yeast.

431
00:28:06,540 --> 00:28:13,160
Yeast is the magical ingredient that converts the sugars into alcohol and carbon dioxide.

432
00:28:14,160 --> 00:28:19,260
Kurt was introduced to the yeast that we use at a small brew pub in Dusseldorf, Germany.

433
00:28:20,160 --> 00:28:26,580
But in 16th century Germany, wheat's esteemed status was grounds for the grain to be banned from beer.

434
00:28:27,300 --> 00:28:35,060
The German purity law, the Reinheitsgebot, dictated that beer could only contain malt, hops, water,

435
00:28:35,061 --> 00:28:37,200
and they didn't know about yeast at that time,

436
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:38,460
but it had to be those ingredients,

437
00:28:38,760 --> 00:28:41,240
and that was specifically malted barley at that point.

438
00:28:41,600 --> 00:28:43,960
And they prescribed that law partially

439
00:28:43,961 --> 00:28:46,700
to restrict people from using wheat in beer

440
00:28:46,701 --> 00:28:49,160
so that there was more wheat available for bread.

441
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:51,760
It wasn't until the 20th century

442
00:28:51,761 --> 00:28:54,580
that brewing wheat beer was once again permitted in Germany.

443
00:28:56,140 --> 00:28:58,080
The Widmer Brothers Brewery houses

444
00:28:58,081 --> 00:29:00,620
six 50-foot-tall fermentation tanks,

445
00:29:01,040 --> 00:29:03,320
each with a 1,500-barrel capacity.

446
00:29:05,060 --> 00:29:08,880
So from here, the beer ferments, and it can be as long as a month,

447
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:10,840
depending on the beer type that we're making.

448
00:29:11,280 --> 00:29:14,260
Woodward-Hefeweizen, the fermentation is a fairly rapid style,

449
00:29:14,380 --> 00:29:15,500
and it's ready in about a week.

450
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:19,860
Once it's finished, it's ready for either packaging into bottles

451
00:29:19,861 --> 00:29:21,980
or racking, which is better than kegging room.

452
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:25,320
Here's where tradition meets technology.

453
00:29:26,240 --> 00:29:30,340
Two robots do all the heavy lifting that guys used to have to do,

454
00:29:30,341 --> 00:29:33,360
but one of the things that the robots do with our beer that's unique

455
00:29:33,361 --> 00:29:39,340
because it's this unfiltered wheat beer is that we flip our kegs because we found that when the

456
00:29:39,341 --> 00:29:43,480
bar owner clips them right side up again that helps mix things up a little bit and keeps the

457
00:29:43,481 --> 00:29:44,714
beer nice and turbid.

458
00:29:44,715 --> 00:29:49,120
Another way to bring out wheat beer's turbidity is with an authentic German

459
00:29:49,121 --> 00:29:49,821
pour.

460
00:29:49,822 --> 00:29:59,040
It's very important when a beer is poured that there be a nice creamy head for the duration

461
00:29:59,041 --> 00:30:01,340
of the beer as a person is drinking it.

462
00:30:01,980 --> 00:30:03,160
And that takes time.

463
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:06,860
And so what I'm going to show you is how you would typically get it

464
00:30:06,861 --> 00:30:10,140
if you were in a small pub in Germany.

465
00:30:11,260 --> 00:30:15,600
Bartender would pour this, walk away, do whatever he has to do.

466
00:30:16,100 --> 00:30:17,340
Head dies down a little bit.

467
00:30:17,440 --> 00:30:18,200
They come back.

468
00:30:18,880 --> 00:30:20,460
And again, I'm pouring right down the center,

469
00:30:20,900 --> 00:30:23,840
and I'm not making any effort to minimize the foam up.

470
00:30:23,841 --> 00:30:25,400
I want it to actually rise up.

471
00:30:25,401 --> 00:30:29,860
And when I'm done, it should be a very thick head

472
00:30:29,861 --> 00:30:31,980
that you could balance a quarter on

473
00:30:31,981 --> 00:30:33,880
that stays there for the whole duration

474
00:30:33,881 --> 00:30:35,260
of the time that you're drinking that beer.

475
00:30:36,540 --> 00:30:37,680
God, that looks great, huh?

476
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:41,760
Who could resist that?

477
00:30:44,700 --> 00:30:46,920
Whether guzzling or gobbling the grain,

478
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:52,340
Americans consume an average of 134 pounds of wheat each year.

479
00:30:53,340 --> 00:30:58,240
As the worldwide population grows, our reliance on wheat only increases.

480
00:30:59,680 --> 00:31:04,320
But some experts fear the fragile crop could vanish in a matter of days.

481
00:31:05,580 --> 00:31:09,500
These scientists have turned to corn to prevent that from happening.

482
00:31:12,360 --> 00:31:19,200
In the last hundred years, the Earth's population has quadrupled to more than 6.5 billion people.

483
00:31:20,060 --> 00:31:21,840
Billions are hungry every day.

484
00:31:22,340 --> 00:31:24,720
and hundreds of millions are in danger of starvation.

485
00:31:26,660 --> 00:31:31,000
Wheat has provided help in times of famine, epidemic disease, and war.

486
00:31:31,740 --> 00:31:34,360
But the future of this saving grain is at stake.

487
00:31:35,900 --> 00:31:40,800
Extreme weather, insects, and fungus threaten the survival of wheat fields worldwide.

488
00:31:40,801 --> 00:31:47,040
And a substantial loss of the wheat crop would be devastating to the global food supply.

489
00:31:47,041 --> 00:31:50,540
Historically, the world has operated usually

490
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:53,440
in excess of 100 days of stored wheat.

491
00:31:53,540 --> 00:31:55,280
So if we just stopped having wheat,

492
00:31:55,380 --> 00:31:58,580
we'd have at least 100 days to feed the world.

493
00:31:58,680 --> 00:32:01,180
And over the last, oh, say 10 years,

494
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:04,520
that capacity has been diminishing down to something

495
00:32:04,620 --> 00:32:07,500
like 36 or 37 days.

496
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:10,300
It is possible that we could have a disease that could

497
00:32:10,400 --> 00:32:14,840
devastate a crop in a matter of 10 or 15 days.

498
00:32:14,940 --> 00:32:16,540
In fact, the staff of life

499
00:32:16,541 --> 00:32:17,900
of life may be in danger.

500
00:32:19,900 --> 00:32:24,080
In March of 2008, a virulent strain of wheat stem rust

501
00:32:24,081 --> 00:32:27,720
called UG-99 that originated in Africa in 1999

502
00:32:27,721 --> 00:32:31,520
spread into Iran, destroying vital wheat fields.

503
00:32:33,160 --> 00:32:38,220
And it's moved through Iran, and it's now threatening

504
00:32:38,221 --> 00:32:40,360
parts of the wheat crop in Pakistan,

505
00:32:40,860 --> 00:32:43,140
and eventually is going to threaten the wheat crops

506
00:32:43,141 --> 00:32:45,580
in India and even in China.

507
00:32:45,581 --> 00:32:51,860
And so it's really critical that breeders find new sources of resistance.

508
00:32:53,180 --> 00:32:57,420
At the Wheat Genetic and Genomic Resource Center at Kansas State University,

509
00:32:58,200 --> 00:33:02,180
scientists hope the key to resistance lies in one of wheat's ancient ancestors.

510
00:33:03,460 --> 00:33:05,860
In their gene bank devoted solely to wheat,

511
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:10,520
more than 2,500 lines of the plant are maintained for such research purposes.

512
00:33:11,960 --> 00:33:14,560
We do a lot of screening for leaf rust resistance.

513
00:33:14,561 --> 00:33:16,560
And we've gone through 500 lines.

514
00:33:16,561 --> 00:33:19,560
And some are very resistant to the disease,

515
00:33:19,561 --> 00:33:22,560
and others are very susceptible to the disease.

516
00:33:22,561 --> 00:33:25,560
So unless you have a lot of lines to screen,

517
00:33:25,561 --> 00:33:31,560
you might not have the resistance that you need to transfer to wheat.

518
00:33:33,560 --> 00:33:35,560
And through the modern science of plant breeding,

519
00:33:35,561 --> 00:33:38,560
experts hope wheat will weather the storm.

520
00:33:38,561 --> 00:33:43,560
AgriPro has the largest wheat breeding program in North America.

521
00:33:43,561 --> 00:33:50,560
At their facility in Junction City, Kansas, it all starts with making crosses in the greenhouse.

522
00:33:50,561 --> 00:33:53,393
Wheat is a self-pollinating plant.

523
00:33:53,394 --> 00:34:00,560
Within each wheat floret are an embryo sac, a stigma, and anthers that contain pollen.

524
00:34:00,561 --> 00:34:05,560
When the floret matures, the anthers open and release the pollen.

525
00:34:05,561 --> 00:34:11,560
It sticks to the stigma and germinates down into the embryo sac to pollinate the plant.

526
00:34:11,561 --> 00:34:15,140
The fertilized embryo ultimately becomes a new wheat kernel.

527
00:34:16,420 --> 00:34:22,860
So what I have to do here to make a cross of wheat is to take the anthers out so I just have the embryo left.

528
00:34:23,860 --> 00:34:26,780
After meticulously removing each minuscule anther,

529
00:34:28,480 --> 00:34:32,340
researchers can then crossbreed the plant with other wheats that have superior qualities,

530
00:34:33,040 --> 00:34:37,280
such as disease resistance, high yields, and shorter growing seasons.

531
00:34:37,281 --> 00:34:42,360
Our goal is to produce around 1,000 to 1,500 crosses a year.

532
00:34:42,680 --> 00:34:47,080
Out of those 1,000 to 1,500, if we can have one to two varieties that make it to market,

533
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:49,660
that will be functional to the farmer.

534
00:34:49,960 --> 00:34:50,920
We are very happy.

535
00:34:51,880 --> 00:34:55,060
And that journey to market takes 10 to 12 years of testing.

536
00:34:56,580 --> 00:35:02,960
Each year, AgriPro technicians plant the experimental varieties in one of their 5 feet by 10 feet test plots.

537
00:35:02,961 --> 00:35:07,400
At harvest, only the best are kept to be replanted the following season.

538
00:35:07,401 --> 00:35:11,601
We have close to 700 different plots of wheat.

539
00:35:11,602 --> 00:35:15,620
We'll come into these plots and we'll select

540
00:35:15,621 --> 00:35:20,016
100 to 150 heads for good disease resistance and plant type.

541
00:35:20,017 --> 00:35:22,580
The purpose is to develop a variety

542
00:35:22,581 --> 00:35:30,359
that has a really good purity. Today, AgriPro offers 40 wheat varieties for farmers.

543
00:35:30,360 --> 00:35:31,100
Although

544
00:35:31,101 --> 00:35:34,631
each cost a whopping $1.2 million to develop.

545
00:35:34,632 --> 00:35:38,240
AgriPro sells about 9 million 50 pound bags of

546
00:35:38,241 --> 00:35:41,414
wheat in the United States and Canada each year.

547
00:35:41,415 --> 00:35:44,720
But perhaps the most notable achievements in wheat

548
00:35:44,721 --> 00:35:51,340
genetics dates back to the 1960s when American plant breeder Norman Borlaug developed and

549
00:35:51,341 --> 00:35:55,983
introduced new wheat varieties to Mexico, India, and Pakistan.

550
00:35:55,984 --> 00:35:58,380
The short high yield wheat saved

551
00:35:58,381 --> 00:35:59,907
millions from starvation.

552
00:35:59,908 --> 00:36:04,060
It was so successful that it came to be called the green revolution.

553
00:36:05,580 --> 00:36:11,500
Now, once again, wheat is in for a big change with the help of an unlikely source, corn.

554
00:36:12,940 --> 00:36:17,740
AgriPro calls it speed breeding because the process takes half as long as traditional methods.

555
00:36:18,940 --> 00:36:23,135
Basically, we're taking a wheat plant and crossing it with a corn plant.

556
00:36:23,136 --> 00:36:24,300
And when we do that,

557
00:36:24,301 --> 00:36:25,800
you get a viable embryo.

558
00:36:26,100 --> 00:36:31,540
That developing embryo recognizes the corn chromosomes as being different.

559
00:36:32,080 --> 00:36:36,880
And so the cells of a wheat plant have a number of enzymes

560
00:36:36,881 --> 00:36:40,800
that are there to attack and destroy the corn chromosomes.

561
00:36:41,460 --> 00:36:45,160
And so what you end up with is a haploid wheat seedling.

562
00:36:45,980 --> 00:36:49,360
Haploid means the wheat embryo only has one set of chromosomes.

563
00:36:50,200 --> 00:36:53,280
So technicians double them through a chemical reaction,

564
00:36:53,281 --> 00:36:55,480
creating a pure wheat embryo.

565
00:36:55,481 --> 00:37:00,760
If they were left unattended, they would normally abort and die.

566
00:37:01,500 --> 00:37:05,800
So we have to go in and very carefully tease out that embryo

567
00:37:05,801 --> 00:37:09,260
and put it on an artificial media.

568
00:37:09,720 --> 00:37:12,380
That has all of the organics.

569
00:37:12,600 --> 00:37:18,040
It allows that young embryo to grow into a plant that produces seeds.

570
00:37:19,200 --> 00:37:22,140
Every kernel of the resulting plant will be genetically identical.

571
00:37:22,141 --> 00:37:26,940
With this and other methods, scientists are betting on a bright future for this vital crop.

572
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:31,340
Breeders are going to be able to find wheat that produces much, much higher yields,

573
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:36,420
that is basically impervious to all types of environmental or biological assaults.

574
00:37:36,940 --> 00:37:39,480
While many researchers strive for super wheat,

575
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:44,340
one company hopes the golden grain could be the answer to saving millions of trees.

576
00:37:52,140 --> 00:37:57,980
Can wheat rock?

577
00:37:58,580 --> 00:38:00,960
A company in Grand Island, Nebraska, thinks so.

578
00:38:01,380 --> 00:38:06,280
We saw a great opportunity to reach out to the world through music, teaching the world

579
00:38:06,281 --> 00:38:07,160
about biodegradables.

580
00:38:07,640 --> 00:38:11,660
We can actually make multiple products, including drumsticks and guitar picks, from different

581
00:38:11,661 --> 00:38:12,740
materials such as wheat.

582
00:38:14,980 --> 00:38:17,840
Wheatware makes products from the entire wheat plant.

583
00:38:17,841 --> 00:38:24,120
Manufacturing starts with bio-resin, a plastic-like material derived from the plant's sugars

584
00:38:24,121 --> 00:38:26,660
that can be molded into a myriad of shapes.

585
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:32,800
But the Wheatware Company is so secretive about its proprietary equipment

586
00:38:32,801 --> 00:38:37,140
that they covered most of it with blue tarps before the Modern Marvels team arrived.

587
00:38:38,140 --> 00:38:43,540
This technology is distinct from plastic injection molding.

588
00:38:43,541 --> 00:38:53,060
Plastic injection molding allows for high temperatures to melt the plastic pellets into a liquid, thereby pressurizing into a mold.

589
00:38:53,420 --> 00:38:55,760
Whereas wheat is more like baking bread.

590
00:38:56,520 --> 00:39:02,200
In baking bread, if you have too high a heat, what you'll do is you'll burn it, which causes it to solidify.

591
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:03,980
This technology is very specific.

592
00:39:06,160 --> 00:39:09,060
And wheatware keeps a very close guard on those specifics.

593
00:39:09,061 --> 00:39:14,960
Beyond their musical instruments, Wheatware offers golf products,

594
00:39:15,060 --> 00:39:19,240
eating utensils, hair combs, clothes hangers,

595
00:39:19,340 --> 00:39:21,680
and chopsticks, to name a few.

596
00:39:21,780 --> 00:39:24,740
We got involved with chopsticks when we heard the statistic

597
00:39:24,840 --> 00:39:28,480
that 25 million trees are clear cut each year to produce

598
00:39:28,580 --> 00:39:31,180
wood-disposable chopsticks.

599
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:35,380
In Japan, they use 65 million chopsticks a day.

600
00:39:35,381 --> 00:39:41,764
day. So we realized that wheat ware is heat resistant, strong, durable and rigid.

601
00:39:41,765 --> 00:39:42,465
The

602
00:39:42,466 --> 00:39:48,800
qualities necessary for a chopstick replacement, we got busy.

603
00:39:48,801 --> 00:39:54,160
While similar products can be made from other crops like corn, wheat provides more durability

604
00:39:54,161 --> 00:39:56,304
and heat resistance.

605
00:39:56,305 --> 00:40:03,700
And this renewable resource will decompose in less than 90 days. That

606
00:40:03,701 --> 00:40:07,780
means golf tees can be left on the course as fertilizer.

607
00:40:07,781 --> 00:40:09,960
If golfing is not your thing, check out

608
00:40:09,961 --> 00:40:14,620
the world's first so-called eco-yo-yo made of wheat.

609
00:40:14,621 --> 00:40:18,280
Wheatware may have their wheat on a string,

610
00:40:18,281 --> 00:40:22,300
but it turns out there are plenty of other uses for the crop.

611
00:40:22,301 --> 00:40:25,860
The inedible parts of the plant, the wheat chap and straw,

612
00:40:25,861 --> 00:40:28,000
make up about half of the yield of the wheat crop.

613
00:40:28,001 --> 00:40:33,000
Many farmers use it as animal feed and bedding.

614
00:40:33,001 --> 00:40:37,000
It can even be transformed into cat litter, soap,

615
00:40:37,001 --> 00:40:39,000
and pillow stuffing.

616
00:40:39,001 --> 00:40:44,000
But one of the oldest uses for wheat straw is adobe construction.

617
00:40:44,001 --> 00:40:48,000
In fact, the first wheat farmers of the Fertile Crescent

618
00:40:48,001 --> 00:40:51,000
also turned to the plant to help build their cities.

619
00:40:51,001 --> 00:40:55,000
Bricks made from mud, clay, water, and straw

620
00:40:55,001 --> 00:40:57,000
are extremely durable.

621
00:40:57,001 --> 00:41:02,460
durable. They can even be found in structures built more than 2,000 years ago.

622
00:41:03,740 --> 00:41:10,680
This is a very archaic method of making a brick. Right now we have sand and clay,

623
00:41:11,080 --> 00:41:13,183
and we're going to add a little straw.

624
00:41:13,184 --> 00:41:16,060
And as times progress, people would put the straw on

625
00:41:16,061 --> 00:41:19,307
a block and chop it and make small fibers.

626
00:41:19,308 --> 00:41:22,940
The smaller the fibers, the stronger the brick.

627
00:41:22,941 --> 00:41:29,540
though the straw makes up only about one-sixth of each brick it's the key binding material

628
00:41:29,541 --> 00:41:35,440
without the tenacity of the wheat straw the bricks would crumble

629
00:41:35,441 --> 00:41:43,720
whether you turn to wheat to build your home quench your thirst or feed your family the fate

630
00:41:43,721 --> 00:41:49,580
of the crop ultimately depends on the brigade of more than 700 custom harvesters making the four

631
00:41:49,581 --> 00:41:54,580
long harvest run from Texas to Canada.

632
00:41:54,581 --> 00:41:58,080
It's a responsibility that custom cutter Ron Meisner has

633
00:41:58,081 --> 00:42:01,580
gladly accepted for 37 years.

634
00:42:01,581 --> 00:42:04,580
It's a very, very tough business.

635
00:42:04,581 --> 00:42:05,580
I've seen a lot of people come.

636
00:42:05,581 --> 00:42:08,580
I've seen a lot of people go.

637
00:42:08,581 --> 00:42:10,580
I will be doing this till the day I die,

638
00:42:10,581 --> 00:42:13,580
and it's going to be hard for them to plant me in the cemetery

639
00:42:13,581 --> 00:42:17,580
because I'm going down in a 9600 John Deere cotton bite.

640
00:42:17,581 --> 00:42:21,660
And the Meisner crew knows it's them versus weather and time.

641
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:24,320
At stake is a harvest of gold.

